[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Named Queries and Stored Procedures in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores how to use Named Queries and Stored Procedures in Hibernate. Both mechanisms allow you to encapsulate SQL or JPQL logic outside your Java code, promoting better maintainability, separation of concerns, and performance optimization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Named Queries in Hibernate"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Named Query is a static query defined with a predefined name, usually using annotations on your entity class. When the application starts, Hibernate parses and validates these queries, which can catch syntax errors early and improve runtime execution speed."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
@NamedQuery(
    name = "User.findByEmail",
    query = "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email"
)
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    // Getters and setters omitted for brevity
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing Named Queries"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once defined, you can retrieve the named query from the EntityManager or Session using its assigned name and supply parameters dynamically."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Query<User> query = session.createNamedQuery("User.findByEmail", User.class);
query.setParameter("email", "john.doe@example.com");
User user = query.uniqueResult();
session.close();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Stored Procedures in Hibernate"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stored procedures allow you to execute database-native routines. Hibernate provides the StoredProcedureQuery interface to call procedures seamlessly across different relational database management systems."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a database procedure named 'get_user_by_id' that takes an 'IN' parameter for the user ID and returns an 'OUT' parameter or result set."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
StoredProcedureQuery query = session.createStoredProcedureQuery("get_user_by_id", User.class);
query.registerStoredProcedureParameter("userId", Long.class, ParameterMode.IN);
query.setParameter("userId", 1L);

List<User> users = query.getResultList();
session.close();"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Named Stored Procedures"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similar to standard named queries, you can map stored procedures directly to your entity classes using the @NamedStoredProcedureQuery annotation."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@NamedStoredProcedureQuery(
    name = "User.totalActiveUsers",
    procedureName = "sp_count_active_users",
    parameters = {
        @StoredProcedureParameter(mode = ParameterMode.OUT, name = "userCount", type = Long.class)
    }
)
@Entity
public class User { ... }"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Summary"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Named Queries for read-heavy operations where logic remains static and benefits from pre-parsed caching."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Stored Procedures when executing complex business logic residing directly within the database engine or for performance-critical batch operations."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close your sessions or utilize dependency injection via EntityManager to prevent memory leaks."}]
